    "notes": "Complete independent small log poultry-house interpretation: slightly irregular mapped walls, white plinth, brown recessed gable door and white-framed divided window, closed timber gables, steep weathered-gray roof with closed eave/fascia thickness, corner log ends and a supported approach stair. Use shared wood_log, wood_plain, wood_vertical, shingle_cedar, plaster_lime and storefront surfaces. The companion red-roof shed remains a separate model; no trees, copied signs, people or photo pixels are baked in. Photo-led wall2.45m, gray pitched ridge4.8m above floor; brown door.98x2.12m at left and white-framed.88x1.13m window at right. Floor 0.9195721926610361m above outside datum and.15m above the highest retained corner. Coarse ground varies.88m across the footprint; exposed white plinth is.15..1.03m and is larger than parts of the photo. 5supported approach treads reconcile the native grade. Roof finish/pitch, course/bay counts, hidden rear and historic thresholds remain approximations. Poultry-house identity and front assignment are inferred from the paired museum photograph, footprint sizes/separation and relative estate context; public map tags name both structures only as sheds. The gray roof follows the photograph despite a generic red-roof map tag. Its shared cedar graph approximates the uncertain historic roof finish. Native ground exposes a larger plinth/stair than the photo; do not treat those levels, course count or thresholds as surveyed. Hidden rear details remain reconstructed; estate service inventory is unfinished."
